  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Some areas of frost tonight. Otherwise, \ngenerally tranquil weather is expected to prevail through the \nearly portion of next week.\n\nHigh pressure will remain in place within the vicinity of our\narea through the middle of next week with continuing mild and \ndry conditions expected.\n\nCooler, more seasonable weather is expected for the weekend. \nHighs will be coolest today, in the upper 50s to low 60s, under\npartly cloudy skies. Partly to mostly cloudy skies from high\ncloud cover expected Sunday, but a bit milder with highs in the\nlow to mid 60s.\n\nLows tonight and Sunday night will be mainly in the low 30s to \nlow 40s. There is increasing probability for some frost\ndevelopment both nights, especially for the remaining PA and NJ\nzones where the growing season remains active (save for Philly \nand DelCo). Temperatures should fall quickly during the evening\ninto the upper 30s and low 40s as winds decouple with the high\npressure center nearby, then continue slowly falling overnight.\nWe do anticipate thickening cirrus late tonight, which should \nhelp put the brakes on runaway radiational cooling. \nNevertheless, some ares of frost development may certainly still\noccur in the usual colder locales prior to the thicker cloud \ncover arriving. Opted to go with a Frost Advisory tonight for\nmost of the remaining PA and NJ active zones. Some patchy frost\nis possible across interior Delmarva as well, but dewpoints and\ntemperatures are forecast to be a few degrees warmer there than\nfarther north.\n\nScattered to broken high cloud cover will persist into early \nnext week despite surface high pressure. Temperatures are \nexpected to increase back to above normal values early next \nweek, with highs in the mid 60s to low 70s and milder \novernights (no frost/freeze potential).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain is expected to return late next week. \n\nThe stretch of dry weather is expected to come to an end late \nnext week. It appears increasingly likely that a period or\nperiods of rainfall will move into the area as a storm system \naffects the region Thursday through at least Friday. Early in \nthe week, a cold front will be moving across the central part of\nthe country. The parent low moves across the Great Lakes and \ninto Canada later in the week, while a secondary low develops \nand moves into the southern states by mid week. This low is \nexpected to strengthen as it moves into the Mid Atlantic and \nnortheast states later in the week, bringing our area a likely\nwindow to end our dry streak. At this time, this appears to be\njust a beneficial rainfall event with no significant weather\nhazards anticipated.",
